; Woven DNS, for import into Cloudflare
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; Cloudflare: your zone > DNS > Records > Import and Export > Import.
; Everything arrives DNS-only (grey cloud), which is what we want: see
; docs/dns/README.md for why, and for the two values you must confirm first.
;
; The TTL of 300 is deliberate while things are moving. Raise it to 3600 once
; the addresses have not changed for a week.
;
; BEFORE IMPORTING, replace the two __RAILWAY_*__ placeholders with the CNAME
; targets Railway shows after you add each custom domain. They are usually the
; service's own *.up.railway.app hostname, but Railway is the authority and a
; guess here fails silently as a certificate error.

$ORIGIN woventechnology.com.
$TTL 300

; --- the site ---------------------------------------------------------------
; Cloudflare flattens a CNAME at the apex, so the bare domain can point at
; Railway without an A record whose address we would then have to chase.
@                       300  IN  CNAME  __RAILWAY_SITE__
www                     300  IN  CNAME  __RAILWAY_SITE__

; --- the public API ---------------------------------------------------------
; Reservations, applications and contact. Holds no household data.
api                     300  IN  CNAME  __RAILWAY_SITE_API__

; --- the relay --------------------------------------------------------------
; Carries sealed frames between a Core and its dashboards and keeps nothing.
; It must stay DNS-only: the connection is a long-lived WebSocket, and a proxy
; in front of it buys nothing and can time it out.
relay                   300  IN  CNAME  __RAILWAY_RELAY__

; --- mail -------------------------------------------------------------------
; No mail is accepted at this domain, and saying so stops the backscatter that
; an unconfigured domain attracts.
@                       300  IN  MX     0 .

; Nothing is authorised to send as this domain yet. When Resend is set up,
; replace this record with the one Resend gives you and add its DKIM record;
; until then a hard fail is the honest and the safer answer.
@                       300  IN  TXT    "v=spf1 -all"

; Reject anything that fails, and hear about it. Change the address to one you
; actually read, or drop the rua tag.
_dmarc                  300  IN  TXT    "v=DMARC1; p=reject; rua=mailto:dmarc@woventechnology.com; aspf=s; adkim=s"

; --- nothing else -----------------------------------------------------------
; No wildcard. A wildcard would answer for every name anyone invents, which is
; how a subdomain nobody meant to publish ends up serving something.
